# Verification verdict — runtime scratch written by /lisa:implement's Verify
# flow and read by the enforce-verification-gate Stop hook. Never committed.
.lisa/verification-status.json

# Freshness-bound standards proof is local runtime evidence, not knowledge.
.lisa/standards/latest.json

# Remote-dispatch ledger. Records the sessions this machine started so a later
# run can find them. Per developer by construction — each fires their own
# routine under their own account — so committing it would collide on every
# dispatch while telling a teammate nothing they can act on.
.lisa/remote-dispatch.json

# Automation run outcomes — local scheduler observations read by
# /lisa:automation-status. Runbooks are project knowledge; run records are not.
.lisa/automations/runs/

# Proof that `lisa apply` completed in THIS checkout, read by `lisa doctor` to
# report a repo that has silently stopped receiving templates. Machine-local by
# construction: committing it would churn on every version bump, conflict
# between developers on different Lisa versions, and lie about a fresh clone
# that has never installed.
.lisa/apply-receipt.json

# What the detached lockfile-reconciliation trampoline actually did, read by
# `lisa doctor`. Machine-local for the same reason as the receipt above: it
# describes this checkout's last install, not the project, and a committed one
# would vouch for a clone that has never installed.
.lisa/reconciliation-report.json

# Repository-readiness report, written by `lisa doctor --readiness`. Derived,
# not knowledge: every field is recomputed from the tree on the next run, and
# three of the nine — generated_at, lisa_version, and worker_signature (the
# host/model/version of the agent runtime that produced it) — describe the RUN,
# not the repository. Committing it conflicts on every pair of branches that ran
# doctor, and no resolution of that conflict is correct: a readiness report is
# one assessment of one tree, so a blend of two describes a tree that never
# existed. Every consumer reads the on-disk file, which is there whether or not
# git tracks it. `lisa doctor` warns when a checkout tracks one anyway, because
# adding this line does not untrack a file already committed
# (CodySwannGT/lisa#3046).
.lisa/readiness.json

# Two-snapshot local temp-growth measurement. It records machine paths, inode
# identity and live process ownership, so it is runtime evidence, not source.
.lisa/tmpdir-growth.json

# Built Claude Code plugins are committed so the GitHub marketplace works.
# Regenerate with: bun run build:plugins

# END: AI GUARDRAILS





.lisa.config.local.json

# Playwright MCP scratch output (never committed; keep out of the evidence manifest)
.playwright-mcp/

# Local document-rendering scratch. `tmp/` holds intermediate render inputs and
# per-page images; `output/` holds the rendered artifacts. Both are reproducible
# from the committed source documents, so committing them would carry megabytes
# of binaries that no code reads — nothing in src/, scripts/, or tests/ resolves
# either path.
tmp/
output/

# Mutation gate working directories. `.stryker-tmp/` is the sandbox copy of the
# repository Stryker mutates in; `reports/mutation/` is the HTML report. Both are
# regenerated by `bun run test:mutation` and must stay untracked — the sandbox is
# a full second copy of the tree, and Stryker copies whatever is not ignored, so
# a tracked sandbox would nest copies of itself on every run.
.stryker-tmp/
reports/mutation/

# Produced in-run by `check:shell-guard-refusals --trace-out`. Never committed:
# a stale trace reports a guard as evidenced long after its driving test was
# deleted, which is the false claim of CodySwannGT/lisa#3931 with a longer half-life.
.lisa/shell-guard-trace.jsonl
